    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">Where Are the Best Photo Spots on a Boat Trip Along Madeira&#x27;s South Coast?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>26 November 2026</span><span>6 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">The best photo spots west of Funchal are Câmara de Lobos bay, the 580 metre wall of Cabo Girão from below, the swim stop at Fajã dos Padres, Ribeira Brava and the old pier at Ponta do Sol. Morning gives the cleanest colour on the cliffs, and the last hour before sunset the warmest.</p>

      <h2>Which spots are worth having your camera ready for?</h2><p>Seven, in the order you meet them heading west from Marina do Funchal. A 2 hour 30 Day Trip passes the first six. Ponta do Sol, the seventh, is only on the 3 hour version.</p><ol><li><p><strong>Funchal from the harbour mouth.</strong> The first two minutes are the ones people miss. As the boat clears the harbour wall, turn round: the whole city rises behind you in one frame, from the yellow fort to Monte. Shoot wide, keep the horizon level, and include a slice of the boat's stern for depth.</p></li><li><p><strong>Câmara de Lobos bay.</strong> About 3.7 nautical miles out, the bay opens on the right. From the water it is a ready-made frame: painted fishing boats on the shore, the small chapel, houses stacked on the rock. Use a short zoom rather than the wide lens, so the town fills the picture instead of a lot of sea.</p></li><li><p><strong>Cabo Girão from below.</strong> The signature shot of the coast. Turn the phone to portrait, use the ultra-wide lens and put something in the bottom of the frame for scale: the bow, a person seen from behind, a paddle board. Without scale, 580 metres of rock looks like any other cliff in a photo.</p></li><li><p><strong>The fajãs under the cliff.</strong> At the foot of Cabo Girão, small fields and a thin cable car line sit on flat ground by the sea. A tight zoom on the huts with the wall above them tells the story of the place in one picture.</p></li><li><p><strong>Fajã dos Padres.</strong> The swim stop is the best place for people pictures. Swimmers in deep blue water, someone jumping off the boat, a paddle board with the green fajã behind. Burst mode helps with the jumps. Shoot slightly down from the deck to get the colour of the water.</p></li><li><p><strong>Ribeira Brava.</strong> The town sits at the mouth of a deep valley, with the church's tiled bell tower and the small 1708 fort on the seafront. From the sea you get the valley climbing inland behind the houses, which you never see from the promenade.</p></li><li><p><strong>Ponta do Sol.</strong> Only on the longer trips. The old stone pier, opened in 1849, has a wide round arch, and the village climbs the cliff behind it. In soft late afternoon light it is one of the prettiest frames on the coast.</p></li></ol><h2>What light works best on this coast?</h2><p>The south coast faces south, so the sun crosses in front of it all day. That makes the light easy to plan around.</p><h3>Morning trips</h3><p>Leaving at 10:00 and heading west, the sun is behind you and to the left. The cliffs are lit from the front and the side, colours are clean and the water looks at its bluest. This is the best light for Cabo Girão and the fajãs.</p><h3>Afternoon trips</h3><p>Leaving at 14:00, the sun moves round ahead of you as the afternoon goes on. Cliffs look more contrasty, and the sea sparkles more, which is great for people shots at the swim stop and harder for wide landscapes. On the way back east the light is behind you again and the coast glows.</p><h3>Sunset</h3><p>Where the sun goes down depends on the month. Seen from the water off Cabo Girão, the December sun sets well south of west, straight into open sea. In June it sets north of west, closer to the line of the coast and the hills of the west of the island. So winter sunsets give you the classic sun on the horizon, and summer sunsets give you the coast in silhouette with the sky lit behind it. Both work. Expose for the sky and let the boat go dark.</p><h2>Do you need to take photos yourself on a Chifbay trip?</h2><p>Not really, and that is the point. Every Chifbay trip already comes with three kinds of pictures: high quality photos from a real camera, a 360 degree video shot on an Insta360, and, if the weather allows, drone shots of the boat from overhead. The drone goes up at Cabo Girão, where only an aerial shot can show the boat as a white dot under the wall. You can read what you get in <a href="/posts/drone-footage-boat-trip-madeira">drone footage from a boat trip in Madeira</a> and in our page on <a href="/posts/360-video-photos-boat-trip-madeira">360 video and photos on board</a>.</p><p>So bring your phone for the moments only you will notice, and put it away for the rest.</p><h2>How do you keep a phone or camera safe on a boat?</h2><p>Salt water and a moving boat are hard on gear. A few habits save most of the trouble:</p><ul><li>Use a wrist strap or a lanyard, especially at the bow and over the swim ladder.</li><li>Keep a cloth in your pocket. Spray dries into a salt film that softens every picture.</li><li>Hold the phone with two hands and keep your knees soft during the fast run back. Sit down when the skipper speeds up.</li><li>Do not change lenses on the open deck. Choose one before you leave.</li><li>Wipe everything with a slightly damp cloth back on land, then dry it.</li></ul><h2>When are the photos disappointing?</h2><p>The sky decides, and nobody controls that. On grey days the cliffs lose their colour and the sea turns slate. Under a thick haze, which can come with calima, the dusty wind from the Sahara, the far coast disappears and Cabo Girão looks flat. In strong wind or rain the drone stays in its case. None of this is a reason to cancel on its own, because a moody sea can make good pictures, but if photos are your main reason for coming, keep your dates flexible and book when the forecast shows a clear sky.</p><p>If you want to plan around the light, the <a href="/hidden-coves-half-day">Day Trip</a> covers all seven spots on the 3 hour version, which goes as far as Ponta do Sol, while the <a href="/sunset-cruise">Sunset Trip</a> is the one for warm light and the city lights on the way home. For land shots to add to your set, see our <a href="/posts/top-10-photo-spots-in-madeira">top 10 photo spots in Madeira</a>.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Can I fly my own drone from the deck?</h3><p>Message us on WhatsApp first, before you book. The trip already includes drone footage when the weather allows.</p><h3>Is morning or afternoon better for photos of Cabo Girão?</h3><p>Morning, usually. The cliff is lit from the front and side, and the water looks at its bluest.</p><h3>Are the photos and videos included in the price?</h3><p>Yes. The 360 video, the drone footage when the weather allows and the camera photos are included on every trip.</p><h3>What lens is best for Cabo Girão from the water?</h3><p>The widest one you have, in portrait orientation, with a person or the bow in the frame for scale.</p>

      <blockquote>Turn round as you clear the harbour wall: the first photo of the trip is the one most people forget to take.</blockquote>
    </article>
